2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] How `echo()` works
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] function echo(...args:any):void{
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 	const lines=[];
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 	for(const arg of args){
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 		const line=toString(arg);
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 		lines.push(line.trimEnd());
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 	}
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 	const output=lines.join(" ").trimEnd();
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 	if(output.length){
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 		outputBuffer.push(output);
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 	}
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] }
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] Each arg is converted to string and joined with spaces, then pushed to `outputBuffer`. Later,
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] `flush()` sends it to console and broadcasts via `slopBroadcast()`.
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] So your line outputs:
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] [RELAY] enabling forge for deepseek-chat
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-10-28 12:00:28 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
