2025-10-30 15:18:36 [simon@midnightblue] for video, can you see any issues that stop selection of front and back cameras
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] [kimi-k2-0905] [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—three small but fatal gaps in the current code prevent ever
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] getting the rear camera:
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. No `facingMode` constraint
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    The call to `getUserMedia` in `streamVideo()` only sets `deviceId`.
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    Without `facingMode: 'environment'` the browser is free to give you the same camera every time.
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Enumeration drops the `facingMode` hint
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    `onEnumerateVideo` builds the `<select>` from `deviceId` + `label`, but never stores the
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] `facingMode` value that the spec guarantees for every videoinput.
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    You therefore can’t reconstruct a constraint that flips the camera later.
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. No “flip” button or re-selection logic
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    Nothing in the UI ever calls `getUserMedia` a second time with a different constraint, so even
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] if you had the metadata you’d never use it.
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Minimal fixes
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] a. Keep the `facingMode` when you enumerate:
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] // in onEnumerateVideo
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] addOption(videoInSelect,
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]           JSON.stringify({deviceId: info.deviceId,
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                           facingMode: info.getCapabilities().facingMode?.[0]}),
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]           info.label);
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] b. Offer two buttons (or a toggle) that explicitly request the desired facing:
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] function getCamera(which){   // which = 'user' | 'environment'
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]   const constraints = {
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]     video: { facingMode: which },
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]     audio: false
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]   };
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]   return navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia(constraints)
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905]          .then(streamVideo);
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] c. If you still want to keep the exact `deviceId` route, store the rear-camera id while enumerating
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] and pass it in a second call—but you must also stop the previous tracks before re-calling
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] `getUserMedia` or the browser will deny the request.
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] With those three changes the rear camera becomes selectable and the code will work on both mobile
2025-10-30 15:18:57 [kimi-k2-0905] and desktop browsers.
